{{!
	Notion skin: the button primitive.

	The skin's component-backed buttons are rendered through this one partial -- the sticky
	header's icon buttons, its wider labelled buttons and its leading button, the collapsed
	search toggle, the add-topic button on a talk page, and any menu entry whose component
	asked to be drawn as a button rather than as a link -- so a change made here reaches all
	of them at once. It includes exactly one other partial, the sibling Icon.

	It is not every button in the skin. PinnableHeader.mustache writes its own pin and unpin
	button elements inline, and Dropdown/Open.mustache composes its own cdx-button class list
	for the dropdown handle; neither passes through here, so a change made here does not
	reach them.

	Data dictionary supplied by NotionComponentButton::getTemplateData():
	string|null href target of the button. A truthy value makes the element an anchor with an
		href, a falsy one a button element; nothing else in the skin selects between the two.
		The component pairs a truthy href with the Codex fake-button classes, so the anchor is
		styled as a button while staying a real link -- followable, middle-clickable and fully
		functional with scripts disabled. It is never a div carrying a button role: the
		element itself is what carries keyboard operability and semantics.
	string class the COMPLETE, pre-composed Codex class list, for example
		`cdx-button cdx-button--weight-quiet cdx-button--icon-only notion-search-toggle`.
		Emitted verbatim: this partial neither appends a class of its own nor filters one out.
		Repeating the Codex base class here would emit it twice, and adding a `notion-`
		prefixed class here would impose it on every button in the skin. Weight, action and
		icon-only decisions consequently live in NotionComponentButton.php and the appearance
		in resources/skins.notion.styles/components/Button.less, which resolves Notion design
		tokens; a button that looks wrong is fixed in one of those two files, never here.
	string|null id value of the `id` attribute. A falsy value omits the attribute rather than
		emitting an empty one.
	{key, value}[] array-attributes any further attributes, as a list of records rather than a
		map so that a Mustache section can iterate it. This is the accessibility and
		instrumentation channel -- `aria-` attributes, `title`, `tabindex`, `data-event-name`
		-- and every record is emitted faithfully, because the sticky header duplicates
		controls that assistive technology can already reach elsewhere and depends on a
		`tabindex` of -1 arriving here to keep those duplicates out of the tab order (T290201).
		The component has already dropped null values and the `id`, `class` and `href` keys,
		each of which is emitted from its own key below, so this loop cannot produce a
		duplicate attribute.
	string|null icon icon-pack name such as `search`, `appearance` or
		`speechBubbleAdd-progressive`, painted by the Icon partial. The enclosing section makes
		the current context the name itself, which is the calling convention Icon.mustache
		documents, and a falsy icon renders nothing at all -- that is how a label-only button
		is expressed.
	string label the button's text. It is ALWAYS wrapped in a span, including on an icon-only
		button: the Codex icon-only modifier is what visually hides that span, and keeping it
		is what leaves the control with an accessible name. Do not make it conditional.

	Two behaviours that belong to the CALLER rather than to this partial, both inherited
	unchanged from the reference implementation it mirrors and both confirmed against the
	compiler rather than assumed:

	A falsy value is not the same as an absent key. The template engine resolves a name it
	cannot find in the current data by walking out to the enclosing context, so rendering this
	partial inside a context that happens to carry one of the names above lets that outer value
	through: a list item with an id lends its id to the button nested in it, producing two
	elements with the same id, and an outer href would go further and turn the button element
	into an anchor.

	Passing an explicit null does NOT stop that. LightnCandy treats a key present with the
	value null exactly as it treats a missing key and walks out to the parent anyway, so
	`'id' => null` in the view model is no defence against an enclosing id. Only a non-null
	value shadows the outer one, which is why the components that render this partial inside a
	menu or list context pass the empty string rather than null for the keys that could
	collide -- the empty string is falsy, so the guards below still omit the attribute, but it
	is present enough to block the lookup from escaping. The remedies, in order of preference:
	pass a non-null value for every colliding name, or do not nest the button inside a context
	carrying those names. Never weaken the guards below, which are what keep empty attributes
	out of the markup.

	The element takes HTML's default button type, which is submit. Nothing in this skin's
	chrome puts a button inside a form, so that default is inert here; a caller that does must
	pass an explicit type through the attributes channel, which keeps this partial usable for
	a genuine submit control as well.

	The opening and the closing tag are each split across a matching pair of `href` sections,
	one plain and one inverted, so the two lines must always be edited together: an anchor
	opened against a button closed is invalid markup that no assertion about the data would
	ever reveal. The comments interleaved below swallow the newline and indentation between
	the parts of this inline element, keeping stray whitespace out of the markup at call sites
	that embed it in a list item or beside another control.

	Every interpolation is escaped -- this partial emits no raw HTML and contains no
	triple-brace expression -- and no colour, size, spacing or inline style appears here.

	Two authoring hazards, both verified against the compiler rather than assumed:

	A Mustache comment ends at the first closing delimiter it meets, so tag syntax is
	described in prose above rather than written out literally. Spelling one out here would
	truncate this comment, leave the remainder to be parsed as markup, and -- because the
	compiler raises on a fault instead of degrading -- take every page rendered by this skin
	down with an unclosed-token error.

	The markup below therefore also begins on the same line as this comment's closing
	delimiter, with no newline between them. A comment occupying whole lines of its own is
	stripped together with its trailing newline only while the file is the template being
	rendered; once the file is pulled in as a partial in an inline position -- which is how
	the menu list item embeds a button inside its list item -- that newline survives and
	becomes a stray text node ahead of the element. Keeping the two on one line makes the
	rendered markup identical in every context and under both the PHP and the JavaScript
	Mustache engine, so a snapshot taken in a unit test still describes the real page.
}}{{#href}}<a href="{{.}}"{{/href}}{{^href}}<button{{/href}} class="{{class}}"{{#id}} id="{{.}}"{{/id}}{{#array-attributes}} {{key}}="{{value}}"{{/array-attributes}}>{{!
	}}{{#icon}}{{>Icon}}{{/icon}}{{!
	}}<span>{{label}}</span>
{{#href}}</a>{{/href}}{{^href}}</button>{{/href}}
